# Token Factory's TLS certificate, pinned so the probes can VERIFY it.
#
# The endpoint presents a self-signed certificate, so the public trust store rejects
# it. The wrong response to that is `verify=False`, which gives up the guarantee that
# the host is the intended one on a connection carrying a bearer token. The right one
# is to trust exactly this certificate and nothing else, which is what pinning it here
# achieves -- verification is stronger than the public-CA default, not weaker.
#
# A certificate is public by construction: the server hands it to every client that
# connects. Committing it discloses nothing.
#
# sha256(DER): 3d9f563957908054aa573eaeaf016064bbd6c8d9584561c3ec5dc56c9c3f07b4
# subject/SAN: CN=token-fac-api.truecorp.co.th, DNS:token-fac-api.truecorp.co.th,
#              IP:10.94.154.102  -- the IP is covered, so connecting by address still
#              verifies and no Host-header override is needed.
# not after:   2027-01-01T12:12:09Z
#
# Rotation BREAKS this pin, deliberately: a changed certificate should stop the probes
# and be re-reviewed, not be trusted silently. Re-pin with
#   python -c "import ssl;print(ssl.get_server_certificate(('10.94.154.102',443)))"

